      "content": "Yes, that is correct. By lowering the activation energy, the catalyst allows the oxidation of ammonia to proceed efficiently.\n\n**Final Answer:** C\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Reaction:** $4\\text{NH}_3 + 5\\text{O}_2 \\xrightarrow{\\text{Pt}} 4\\text{NO} + 6\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$\n* **What the terms mean:**\n  * $\\text{NH}_3$: Ammonia gas, the starting material.\n  * $\\text{Pt}$: Platinum (or platinum-rhodium gauze), the catalyst that speeds up the reaction.\n  * $\\text{NO}$: Nitrogen monoxide, the product of the first step of the Ostwald process.\n* **Why we use it:** We use Platinum because it is highly effective at providing a surface for the ammonia and oxygen molecules to react, significantly increasing the rate of reaction without being consumed itself.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Don't confuse your catalysts! Remember the 'Big Three': \n1. **H**aber = **I**ron (HI!)\n2. **C**ontact = **V**anadium (CV)\n3. **O**stwald = **P**latinum (OP)"
